<header class="ed-hero bg-inverse pb-14 pt-32 text-inverse-ink md:pt-40"><div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6"><p class="mb-4 text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-accent">The Naked Tech field guide</p><h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight md:text-5xl">Fake virus pop-ups: close the warning and check browser permissions</h1><p class="ed-deck">Use the browser’s own controls, not a pop-up’s instructions, and remember that closing a warning does not prove the computer is clean.</p></div></header>
<article class="ed-guide bg-canvas py-12 md:py-16" aria-label="Fake virus pop-ups: close the warning and check browser permissions"><div class="ed-layout">
{{ contents([{"id": "start", "title": "Find your starting point"}, {"id": "close-with-browser-controls", "title": "Close with browser controls"}, {"id": "review-one-browser-setting", "title": "Review one browser setting"}, {"id": "decide-the-next-step", "title": "Decide what the warning means next"}, {"id": "related", "title": "What to read next"}]) }}
<div class="ed-reading guide-reading text-lg">
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Fake virus pop-ups are designed to make a web page feel like an emergency. They may imitate a Windows or Apple alert, use a fake scan animation, show a phone number or place a large button over the page. A website can also have permission to send notifications that appear on the desktop. These signs are different from a report inside genuine installed security software. You can close the warning without calling, paying or installing anything, then check the browser permission that may have allowed it.</p>
<section class="ed-start" id="start" aria-labelledby="start-heading"><p class="ed-label">Start here</p><h2 id="start-heading">Start with the browser, not the pop-up</h2><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Close the message using the browser window controls, then review one relevant site permission.</p><div class="ed-choices">
{{ choice("Close the browser warning", "Ignore fake buttons and use the browser’s own tab or window controls.", "#close-with-browser-controls", "startup", "/img/guides/malware-checks-v2.webp", "A conceptual browser window with a small warning triangle.", "Read this part") }}
{{ choice("Check the source permission", "Review the browser’s notification or pop-up setting and block the specific offending site without returning to the suspicious page.", "#review-one-browser-setting", "applications", "/img/guides/malware-checks-v2.webp", "A folder and files beside a magnifying glass.", "Read this part") }}
{{ choice("Record what persists", "Note whether the warning returns, whether an extension or installed tool is involved, and what you changed.", "#decide-the-next-step", "online", "/img/guides/malware-checks-v2.webp", "A notebook, pencil and clock for recording a result.", "Read this part") }}
</div></section>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="close-with-browser-controls"><span aria-hidden="true">01</span>Close with browser controls</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Do not phone, pay, install software or grant remote access because a pop-up tells you to. Fake close buttons can open another page or download. Use the browser’s own close control, such as the tab’s close button or the window control. If a Safari page keeps returning on a Mac, Apple supports quitting Safari and holding Shift while reopening it so the previous page is not restored.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If the message is a desktop notification rather than an open page, do not follow it. The appearance alone does not prove that installed security software found anything. Keep the operating system’s genuine security notifications enabled; the goal is to remove a misleading website permission, not silence all warnings.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Apple’s official guidance covers deceptive pop-ups, fake buttons and relevant Safari settings: <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.apple.com/en-au/102524">Apple Support: avoid deceptive pop-ups and unwanted software</a>.</p>{% call note("care", "Take care", "Do not use the pop-up’s phone number") %}<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">A professional-looking warning can still be a scam. Use a bank, software maker or support provider’s official website or application when you need help.</p>{% endcall %}
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="review-one-browser-setting"><span aria-hidden="true">02</span>Review one browser setting</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">In desktop Chrome, open Settings, then Privacy and security, then Site settings, then Notifications. Check which site is sending the unwanted notification. Under “Not allowed to send notifications”, choose Add and enter that site’s address, following the current Chrome prompts. Do not revisit the suspicious site to change its permission; use the settings page instead. Chrome’s notification controls manage site permission. They are not a malware scanner.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">In Safari on a Mac, open Settings, then Websites, then Pop-up Windows, and set the relevant site to Block if it is listed. In Safari’s Security settings, keep “Warn when visiting a fraudulent website” enabled. Settings names can vary slightly by browser version, so use the browser’s own help if a label differs.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Inspect extensions by name and source. Only disable a clearly unwanted extension when you understand what it is and why it is being changed. Do not perform a bulk reset or remove everything unfamiliar just because a pop-up was alarming. If the browser appears managed unexpectedly, seek help rather than trying to bypass management.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Sources: <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.google.com/chrome/answer/3220216?hl=en">Chrome notification permissions</a> and <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.apple.com/en-au/102524">Apple pop-up guidance</a>.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="decide-the-next-step"><span aria-hidden="true">03</span>Decide what the warning means next</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Closing the pop-up and blocking a permission are useful reversible steps, but they do not prove the computer is clean. If a separately installed security tool reports a detection, follow that tool’s supported information and seek help if the action is unclear. If the problem is persistent after the browser checks, an assessment may be more useful than repeated clicking.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Be more cautious if you installed software from the warning, gave someone remote access, entered a password or shared payment information. Use <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/suspected-scam-first-steps/">suspected scam: first steps</a> for the money or account concern. Record what happened and contact the relevant provider through official details.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Persistent pop-ups, redirects or unwanted extensions can be symptoms of unwanted software, but they are not proof by themselves. Keep updates and Safe Browsing or equivalent protections enabled, and use trusted software sources.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Source: <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.google.com/chrome/answer/2765944?hl=en-gb">Google Chrome guidance on unwanted software symptoms</a>.</p>{% call note("care", "Take care", "Persistence needs context") %}<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">A returning message may be a permission, extension or installed-software issue. Record the pattern and get appropriate help instead of installing another “cleaner” from an advertisement.</p>{% endcall %}
